{
  "name": "Heartbeat",
  "created": "2026-09-13T18:00:00.000Z",
  "notes": "A heart at the middle of the strip: lub, then dub a sixth of a second later, on the beat of whatever is playing -- and a resting pulse when nothing is.\n\nThe lub is the `beat` tracker's pulse. The dub is a `delay` re-firing the beat's edge into an envelope, which is what a delay is for: an envelope alone restarts on a new edge and loses the one in flight. `tempo` shortens the gap as the song speeds up, so a fast track beats tight and a slow one lazily.\n\n`beat` reads nothing until it has locked -- seven to ten beats of music with a clear kick -- and nothing at all in a quiet room. So a `periodic` at 60 BPM runs the same lub-dub chain as a resting heart, scaled by Resting Pulse, and `math max` takes whichever of the two is stronger for each beat. With music playing and Resting Pulse up you get both, slightly out of step; turn it down when the room is loud.",
